= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The following is a <logical derivation> using only A1, A2 and the <modus ponens rule>. The first two lines are assumptions:
$$
\begin{array}{rll}
1.&\alpha\to\beta&\text{assumption},\\
2.&\beta\to\gamma&\text{assumption},\\
3.&(\beta\to\gamma)\to(\alpha\to(\beta\to\gamma))&\mathrm{A1},\\
4.&\alpha\to(\beta\to\gamma)&\mathrm{MP}\ (2,3),\\
5.&(\alpha\to(\beta\to\gamma))\to((\alpha\to\beta)\to(\alpha\to\gamma))&\mathrm{A2},\\
6.&(\alpha\to\beta)\to(\alpha\to\gamma)&\mathrm{MP}\ (4,5),\\
7.&\alpha\to\gamma&\mathrm{MP}\ (1,6).
\end{array}
$$
Thus $\boxed{\{\alpha\to\beta,\beta\to\gamma\}\vdash\alpha\to\gamma}$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

An instance of A3 is
$$
(\neg\alpha\to\neg\beta)\to(\beta\to\alpha).
$$
The second assumed theorem and the <modus ponens rule> therefore give $\Gamma\vdash\beta\to\alpha$. Apply the preceding implication-composition derivation to this theorem and $\Gamma\vdash\neg\alpha\to\beta$. We obtain $\Gamma\vdash\neg\alpha\to\alpha$. The permitted theorem $(\neg\alpha\to\alpha)\to\alpha$ and one more <modus ponens rule> application yield $\boxed{\Gamma\vdash\alpha}$.

