= Solution
{parent=a}

A <first-order structure> for this language is $\mathcal A=(A,f^{\mathcal A})$, where $A$ is a **nonempty set** and $f^{\mathcal A}:A\to A$ is a total <function>. The logical equality symbol is interpreted as actual <equality> on $A$; there are no other nonlogical symbols to interpret.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use an <involution with exactly one fixed point>:
$$
\boxed{\phi=\forall x\,f(f(x))=x\ \land\ \exists z\bigl(f(z)=z\land\forall x\,(f(x)=x\to x=z)\bigr).}
$$
The first conjunct makes every orbit of the <function> $f$ have size one or two. The second says precisely one orbit has size one. A finite model therefore has $1+2m$ elements, an odd number.

Conversely, on any set of size $2m+1$, fix one element and partition the remaining $2m$ elements into pairs; let $f$ swap the two members of each pair. This defines a model of $\phi$. The case $m=0$ gives a one-element model. Thus the finite model sizes are **exactly the positive odd integers**.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Extend the <first-order variable assignment> recursively to <first-order terms> by
$$
\widetilde v(x_i)=v(x_i),\qquad\widetilde v(f(t))=f^{\mathcal A}(\widetilde v(t)).
$$
For atomic formulas, $\mathcal A\models t=s[v]$ exactly when $\widetilde v(t)=\widetilde v(s)$. Extend to the Boolean connectives by their <truth tables>, for example implication is false exactly when its antecedent is true and its consequent false. Quantifiers use modified <first-order variable assignments>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A\models\forall x_i\,\theta[v]&\iff\text{for every }a\in A,\quad\mathcal A\models\theta[v(x_i\mapsto a)],\\
\mathcal A\models\exists x_i\,\theta[v]&\iff\text{for some }a\in A,\quad\mathcal A\models\theta[v(x_i\mapsto a)].
\end{aligned}
$$
This recursion defines <satisfaction for a set structure> for every <first-order formula>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Semantic consequence under an assignment> means that for every <first-order structure> $\mathcal A$ with the relevant <first-order variable assignment> $v$ into its domain,
$$
\boxed{\bigl(\mathcal A\models\gamma[v]\text{ for every }\gamma\in\Gamma\bigr)\ \Longrightarrow\ \mathcal A\models\phi[v].}
$$
More explicitly, in a fixed structure this is the satisfaction implication at $v$; logical consequence requires that implication in all structures and assignments. An assignment takes values in a particular domain, so a single set map is not silently shared between unrelated domains.

By induction on <first-order formulas>, their <truth values> depend only on the values of their <free variables>. A <first-order sentence> has none. If all formulas here are sentences, every displayed satisfaction statement, and therefore the consequence condition, is independent of $v$.

