= Solution
{parent=i}

The antecedent says that the nonempty domain has **at most two elements**: its two witnesses need not be distinct. The consequent says it has exactly one element. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A\models\psi\iff |A|\ne2.}
$$
One-element structures satisfy both sides; domains of size at least three, including infinite domains, make the antecedent false. The <function> $f$ has no bearing on this condition.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A two-element model of $\phi$ would be a finite model with even size, contrary to the defining property established in part (b). Every model of $\phi$ therefore has size other than two, and the preceding interpretation of $\psi$ gives $\boxed{\{\phi\}\models\psi}$. This includes infinite models, since the antecedent of $\psi$ is false there.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <compactness theorem> states that a set of <first-order sentences> has a model if every finite subset has a model.

For each $n\geq1$, let $\chi_n$ assert that at least $n$ distinct elements exist. Consider $T=\{\phi\}\cup\{\chi_n:n\geq1\}$. A finite subset requires only some finite lower bound $N$ on the size. Choose an odd integer $m\geq N$; the given property of $\phi$ supplies a model of that size, satisfying the finite subset. By the <compactness theorem>, $T$ has a model. Its domain has at least $n$ elements for every $n$, so **$\phi$ has an infinite model**. This argument applies to every sentence with the finite-model-size property in (b), irrespective of its behavior on infinite structures.

