= Solution
{parent=a}

A <logical derivation> is a finite proof in the specified calculus, with conclusion $\Gamma\vdash\phi$. Assumptions from $\Gamma$ and instances of A1–A7 are available; the rules are the <modus ponens rule>, <universal generalization rule>, and <weakening>.

In judgement form the <universal generalization rule> takes $\Delta\vdash\theta$ to $\Delta\vdash\forall x_i\theta$ only if $x_i$ is not free in any undischarged assumption in $\Delta$. <Weakening> permits $\Delta\vdash\theta$ to become $\Delta\cup\Sigma\vdash\theta$. All instances of A4 and A5 must meet their stated substitution and <free variable> conditions. **Derivability means existence of such a finite proof with assumptions contained in $\Gamma$.** Equivalently one tracks the finite assumption set used by each proof line.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <first-order term> $t$ is <free for substitution> for $x_i$ in $\phi$ if, at every free occurrence of $x_i$ to be replaced, no enclosing quantifier binds a variable occurring in $t$. Equivalently, the substitution introduces no <variable capture>. Only free occurrences of $x_i$ are replaced.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take $\phi=\forall x_1\,(x_0=x_1)$. The term $x_2$ is <free for substitution> for $x_0$: its variable is not bound by the enclosing quantifier. The term $x_1$ is not <free for substitution> for $x_0$, because insertion under $\forall x_1$ captures that occurrence.

Thus the first substitution gives $\forall x_1(x_2=x_1)$; the second, illegitimate substitution would give $\forall x_1(x_1=x_1)$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In a two-element <first-order structure>, $\phi=\forall x_1(x_0=x_1)$ is false for every <first-order variable assignment>. Blind substitution of $x_1$ for $x_0$ yields $\forall x_1(x_1=x_1)$, which is true. This does not express the original property evaluated at the value of the inserted term: it has changed a free parameter into a <bound variable>.

For a direct failure of the substitution axiom, use $\theta(x_0)=\exists x_1(x_0\ne x_1)$. On a two-element domain, $\forall x_0\theta(x_0)$ is true, whereas the captured instance $\exists x_1(x_1\ne x_1)$ is false. Consequently an unrestricted A4 would prove a false implication. **The <free for substitution> condition is necessary for soundness.**

= c
{parent=3}
