= Solution
{parent=i}

A <well-order> is a strict total order in which every nonempty subset has a least element. A <von Neumann ordinal> is a <transitive set> well-ordered by membership. Thus its predecessors are precisely its members, and its ordering is the restriction of $\in$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $\alpha=\bigcup X$. Every element of $\alpha$ belongs to some <ordinal> in $X$ and is itself an <ordinal>. If $u\in v\in\alpha$, choose $\beta\in X$ with $v\in\beta$; transitivity of $\beta$ gives $u\in\beta\subseteq\alpha$. Thus $\alpha$ is a <transitive set> of <ordinals>.

Any two <ordinals> are comparable by membership or equality, so membership strictly totally orders $\alpha$. Every nonempty subset $A\subseteq\alpha$ has an $\in$-minimal element by the <axiom of foundation>, and totality makes it the least element. Hence **$\bigcup X$ is an <ordinal>**, including $\bigcup\varnothing=0$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <transfinite induction>. For any $\beta<\alpha$, the induction hypothesis and strict increase give $\beta\leq F(\beta)<F(\alpha)$. Thus every $\beta<\alpha$ belongs to $F(\alpha)$, so $\alpha\subseteq F(\alpha)$, i.e.
$$
\boxed{\alpha\leq F(\alpha).}
$$
This includes $\alpha=0$ and does not yet use continuity of the <normal function on an ordinal>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By the <natural-number recursion theorem>, let $\alpha_0=0$ and $\alpha_{n+1}=F(\alpha_n)$. These <ordinals> form a nondecreasing sequence by the preceding bound. If $\alpha_{n+1}=\alpha_n$ for some $n$, it is already a fixed point.

Otherwise the sequence is strictly increasing. Its supremum $\lambda=\bigcup_{n<\omega}\alpha_n$ is a <limit ordinal>, and the sequence is cofinal in it. Continuity and monotonicity give
$$
F(\lambda)=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}F(\beta)=\sup_{n<\omega}F(\alpha_n)=\sup_{n<\omega}\alpha_{n+1}=\lambda.
$$
For the middle equality, every $\beta<\lambda$ is below some $\alpha_n$, while every $\alpha_n<\lambda$. Therefore **$F$ has a fixed point**. The sequence is a set by <axiom schema of replacement>, since $F$ is a definable <set-theoretic class function>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Repeat the preceding construction starting at $\alpha_0=\gamma+1$, for any given <ordinal> $\gamma$. A stabilized value or the strictly increasing sequence's supremum is a fixed point at least $\gamma+1$. Thus fixed points occur above every <ordinal>.

If the fixed points formed a set $S$, their union would be an <ordinal> bounding them all. Applying the construction above that bound contradicts it. Hence
$$
\boxed{\{\alpha\in\mathrm{ON}:F(\alpha)=\alpha\}\text{ is a proper class}.}
$$
This is the unboundedness part of the <fixed point of a normal ordinal function> theorem.

